window.__hvCxRegister("symp", {"v":1,"dialogue":"symp","title":"Symposium","speechLabel":"On this speech","eras":[{"id":"early-empire","label":"Early Roman Empire"},{"id":"late-antiquity","label":"Late antiquity"},{"id":"byzantine","label":"Byzantium"},{"id":"renaissance","label":"Renaissance"},{"id":"modern","label":"Modern"}],"items":[{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":1,"ref":"I.1","t":"The banquet renewed in Plato’s honour","x":"Plato, the father of philosophers, having completed the eighty-first year of his age, died on the seventh of November, his own birthday, as he reclined at a banquet after the dishes had been cleared away. This banquet, which …","tier":"speech","r":[[860,890]],"rs":"172a–178a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":323,"ref":"VII.1","t":"Conclusion of what has been said above, and the opinion of the philosopher Guido Cavalcanti","x":"Last of all Cristoforo Marsuppini, a most cultivated man, who was to take the part of Alcibiades, turned to me with these words. I warmly congratulate, Marsilio, the family of your Giovanni, which, among many knights most …","tier":"dialogue","r":[[860,1119]],"rs":"the whole dialogue"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":4,"ref":"I.2","t":"By what rule Love is to be praised; what his dignity and greatness are","x":"A most welcome lot has fallen to me today, excellent guests, by which it has come about that I should play the part of Phaedrus of Myrrhinus: that Phaedrus, I mean, whose friendship Lysias of Thebes, the consummate orator, valued …","tier":"passage","r":[[890,890]],"rs":"178a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":10,"ref":"I.3","t":"The origin of Love out of Chaos","x":"Orpheus in the Argonautica, when he sang of the beginnings of things in the presence of Chiron and the heroes, followed the theology of Hermes Trismegistus: he set Chaos before the world, and placed Love in the bosom of that …","tier":"passage","r":[[891,891]],"rs":"178b"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":21,"ref":"I.4","t":"On the usefulness of Love","x":"So much for his origin and nobility; I think we must now speak of his usefulness. To recount one by one the benefits Love has conferred on mankind would be needless, especially since they can all be summed up briefly. For they …","tier":"passage","r":[[892,901]],"rs":"178c–180b"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":33,"ref":"II.1","t":"God is goodness, beauty and justice; beginning, middle and end","x":"The Pythagorean philosophers held that the trinity is the measure of all things, for this reason, I think: that God governs things by the number three, and things themselves are also bounded by the number three. Hence that saying …","tier":"speech","r":[[902,927]],"rs":"180c–185c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":37,"ref":"II.2","t":"How the divine beauty gives birth to love","x":"Now this divine beauty has begotten love, that is, desire for itself, in all things. For if God seizes the world to himself and the world is seized, there is one continuous attraction, beginning from God, passing into the world …","tier":"speech","r":[[902,927]],"rs":"180c–185c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":43,"ref":"II.3","t":"Beauty is the splendour of the divine goodness, and God is the centre of four circles","x":"Nor was it without reason that the ancient theologians placed goodness at the centre and beauty in the circle: goodness, I say, at a single centre, but beauty in four circles. The one centre of all things is God; the four circles …","tier":"speech","r":[[902,927]],"rs":"180c–185c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":51,"ref":"II.4","t":"How Plato is to be expounded on divine things","x":"This mystery Plato signified in the letter to King Dionysius, when he affirmed that God is the cause of all beautiful things, as being the principle and origin of all beauty. \"Around the king of all,\" he says, \"are all things …","tier":"speech","r":[[902,927]],"rs":"180c–185c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":58,"ref":"II.5","t":"The divine beauty shines through all things and is loved in all things","x":"But to gather many things into a few words: the good is said to be the supereminent existence of God itself; beauty, a certain act or ray penetrating from there through all things: first into the Angelic Mind, secondly into the …","tier":"speech","r":[[902,927]],"rs":"180c–185c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":60,"ref":"II.6","t":"The passions of lovers","x":"Hence it comes about that the lover's onrush is not quenched by the sight or touch of any body. For he does not desire this body or that; he wonders at, strives after and stands amazed at the splendour of the light from above …","tier":"speech","r":[[902,927]],"rs":"180c–185c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":71,"ref":"II.8","t":"An exhortation to love. On simple love and on mutual love","x":"But you, my friends, I urge and beg to embrace love with all your strength, for it is certainly a divine thing. And do not let that saying frighten you which they report Plato to have made about a certain lover: \"That lover,\" he …","tier":"speech","r":[[902,927]],"rs":"180c–185c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":64,"ref":"II.7","t":"On the two kinds of love and on the twofold Venus","x":"Next we must discuss briefly the two kinds of love. In Plato, Pausanias asserts that Cupid is the companion of Venus, and he thinks it necessary that there be as many Cupids as there are Venuses. Now he mentions two Venuses …","tier":"passage","r":[[903,907]],"rs":"180d–181c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":81,"ref":"II.9","t":"What lovers seek","x":"But what do these seek when they love one another? They seek beauty. For love is the desire of enjoying beauty. And beauty is a certain splendour that seizes the human soul to itself. The beauty of the body is surely nothing …","tier":"passage","r":[[921,927]],"rs":"184b–185c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":89,"ref":"III.2","t":"Love is the author and preserver of all things","x":"The second part of our speech, in which Love was called the maker and preserver of all things, is proved as follows. The craving to propagate one's own perfection is a kind of love. Absolute perfection lies in the supreme power …","tier":"speech","r":[[929,944]],"rs":"185e–188e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":104,"ref":"III.4","t":"No member of the world hates another member","x":"If this is so, the members of this work can in no way be enemies to one another. For fire does not flee water out of hatred of water, but out of love of itself, lest it be put out by the coldness of the water. Nor does water put …","tier":"speech","r":[[929,944]],"rs":"185e–188e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":85,"ref":"III.1","t":"Love is in all things and towards all things, creator of all, master of all","x":"Next, it seems, we must treat three things according to the mind of our Eryximachus. First, that Love, implanted in all things, reaches through them all. Second, that he is the maker and preserver of all the works that are …","tier":"passage","r":[[930,931]],"rs":"186a–b"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":95,"ref":"III.3","t":"That he is the master and governor of the arts","x":"It remains after this to explain how Love is the master and governor of all the arts. We shall understand that he is the master of the arts if only we consider that no one can ever discover or learn any art unless the pleasure of …","tier":"passage","r":[[931,943]],"rs":"186b–188d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":109,"ref":"IV.1","t":"The text of Plato on the ancient nature of men is recounted","x":"With these words our friend made an end of speaking. After him came Cristoforo Landino, a man of outstanding learning, whom we have known as the foremost Orphic and Platonic poet of our times; and he went on in this way, to …","tier":"passage","r":[[947,968]],"rs":"189c–193d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":117,"ref":"IV.3","t":"Man is the soul itself, and the soul is immortal","x":"Since body consists of matter and quantity, and since to matter it belongs to receive, and to quantity only to be divided and extended, and since receiving and division are thus passions, it follows that body is by its own nature …","tier":"speech","r":[[947,968]],"rs":"189c–193d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":113,"ref":"IV.2","t":"Plato's opinion on the ancient figure of man is expounded","x":"These things Aristophanes tells, and very many others, like monsters and portents; and beneath them, as beneath certain veils, we must suppose that divine mysteries lie hidden. For it was the custom of the ancient theologians to …","tier":"passage","r":[[948,968]],"rs":"189d–193d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":126,"ref":"IV.5","t":"By how many ways the soul returns to God","x":"This pride, plainly, is what Aristophanes says was the cause that the soul, which was born whole, was cut; that is, that of its twin lights it thereafter used the one and neglected the other. Plunged into the gulf of the body as …","tier":"passage","r":[[950,965]],"rs":"190a–193a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":123,"ref":"IV.4","t":"That the soul is adorned with two lights, and why it descends into the body. The creation of the soul with two lights","x":"Now the soul, as soon as it is born of God, turns by a certain natural instinct towards God its parent, just as fire, begotten on earth by the power of the higher things, is at once carried by the impulse of its nature towards …","tier":"passage","r":[[951,955]],"rs":"190b–191a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":133,"ref":"IV.6","t":"Love leads souls back to heaven, distributes the degrees of blessedness, and bestows everlasting joy","x":"This god, then, most excellent guests, who Aristophanes says is beneficent to the human race beyond the other gods, you must make gracious to yourselves with every kind of sacrifice; call on him with devout prayers; embrace him …","tier":"passage","r":[[965,968]],"rs":"193a–d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":138,"ref":"V.1","t":"Love is most blessed, because he is beautiful and good","x":"Carlo Marsuppini, sweet nursling of the Muses, following the poet Landino, takes up the speech of Agathon thus. Our Agathon judges the god Love to be most blessed, because he is most beautiful and best. He carefully counts over …","tier":"passage","r":[[974,975]],"rs":"194e–195a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":154,"ref":"V.3","t":"Beauty is something incorporeal","x":"Since this is so, beauty must be something common to virtue, shape and voices. For we should not call each of these three beautiful alike, unless one definition of beauty were present in all three. Hence the reason of beauty …","tier":"speech","r":[[974,989]],"rs":"194e–197e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":162,"ref":"V.4","t":"Beauty is the splendour of the divine countenance","x":"The divine power, which towers above all things, as soon as the angels and souls are born from it, mercifully pours into them, as into its children, that ray of its own in which there is the fruitful power of all things that are …","tier":"speech","r":[[974,989]],"rs":"194e–197e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":170,"ref":"V.5","t":"How love and hatred are born, and that beauty is incorporeal","x":"From all this it follows that all the grace of the divine countenance, which is called universal beauty, is incorporeal not only in the Angel and in the soul, but also in the sight of the eyes. And we love not only this whole …","tier":"speech","r":[[974,989]],"rs":"194e–197e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":175,"ref":"V.6","t":"How many things are required for a thing to be beautiful, and that beauty is a spiritual gift","x":"What, then, is the beauty of the body? A certain act, liveliness and grace, shining in it by the influx of its Idea. Brightness of this kind does not descend into matter before the matter has been most fitly prepared. And the …","tier":"speech","r":[[974,989]],"rs":"194e–197e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":142,"ref":"V.2","t":"How Love is painted, and by which parts of the soul beauty is known and love is begotten","x":"After this Agathon counts over at greater length the things required for the beautiful appearance of this god. He is young, he says, tender, nimble, well-proportioned and shining. We must ask first what these contribute to …","tier":"passage","r":[[975,981]],"rs":"195a–196b"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":183,"ref":"V.7","t":"On the portrait of Love","x":"Agathon, poet that he is, after the manner of the ancient poets clothes this god in a human likeness; and he paints him as beautiful in the way that men are: young, tender, supple or nimble, well composed and shining. To what …","tier":"passage","r":[[975,981]],"rs":"195a–196b"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":196,"ref":"V.10","t":"Love is both older and younger than the other gods","x":"But before I make an end, excellent gentlemen, I shall resolve, as far as my wit allows, three questions that arise in Agathon's discourse. First, it is asked why Phaedrus said that Love is older than Saturn and Jupiter, but …","tier":"passage","r":[[975,977]],"rs":"195a–c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":199,"ref":"V.11","t":"Love reigns before Necessity","x":"Now, to resolve the second question: Love must be said to reign before Necessity for this reason, that the divine Love gave their origin to all things born from him; and in him no violence of necessity is placed. For since he has …","tier":"passage","r":[[977,977],[986,986]],"rs":"195c; 197b"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":209,"ref":"V.12","t":"How, under the reign of Necessity, Saturn castrated Heaven, and Jupiter bound Saturn","x":"How it is that, under the dominion of Necessity, the later gods are said by Agathon here to castrate or to bind their parents, we easily understand from what has been said. For these things are not to be taken to mean that the …","tier":"passage","r":[[977,977]],"rs":"195c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":187,"ref":"V.8","t":"On the virtue of Love","x":"What Agathon says of the four virtues, to signify the goodness of Love, is also to be taken in this way: he is declared just, because where love is whole and true, there too is an exchange of goodwill, which admits no insult or …","tier":"passage","r":[[981,986]],"rs":"196b–197b"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":212,"ref":"V.13","t":"Which gods bestow which arts on men","x":"Agathon holds that the arts were handed down to the human race by the gods on account of love: the art of ruling by Jupiter; of archery, divination and healing by Apollo; work in bronze by Vulcan; the craft of weaving by Minerva …","tier":"passage","r":[[985,986]],"rs":"197a–b"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":193,"ref":"V.9","t":"On the gifts of Love","x":"What Love is has appeared from that discussion of ours; what he is like, from the words of Agathon above. What gifts he bestows is easily learned from what has been said. For one love is simple, the other mutual. The simple love …","tier":"passage","r":[[987,989]],"rs":"197c–e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":218,"ref":"VI.1","t":"Introduction to the discussion of love","x":"Thus far Carlo Marsuppini. Then Tommaso Benci, a careful imitator of Socrates, turned with a willing mind and a cheerful face to the exposition of Socrates' words. Our Socrates, he said, judged by the oracle of Apollo the wisest …","tier":"passage","r":[[990,1009]],"rs":"198a–201e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":237,"ref":"VI.6","t":"How we are caught by love","x":"What I shall say of one case as an example, understand of the other three. Whatever soul slips down into an earthly body under the rule of Jupiter conceives for itself, in the descent itself, a certain definite figure for the …","tier":"speech","r":[[1008,1060]],"rs":"201d–212a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":290,"ref":"VI.13","t":"The light of truth is in the soul","x":"In what way reasons of this kind are in the soul seems, in Plato, to be left doubtful. Anyone who follows the books Plato wrote as a younger man, the Phaedrus, the Meno and the Phaedo, will perhaps think that they are painted on …","tier":"speech","r":[[1008,1060]],"rs":"201d–212a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":221,"ref":"VI.2","t":"Love is midway between beauty and ugliness, and between god and man","x":"The lodestone puts into iron a certain quality of its own, by which the iron, made very like the lodestone, inclines towards this stone. Such an inclination, in so far as it arises from the stone and tends towards the stone, is …","tier":"passage","r":[[1009,1014]],"rs":"201e–202e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":234,"ref":"VI.5","t":"On the order of the Venereal daemons, and how they shoot love","x":"Now the Venereal daemon is a threefold love. The first is placed by the Platonists in the heavenly Venus, that is, in the very intelligence of the Angelic Mind. The second is in the common Venus, that is, in the power of …","tier":"speech","r":[[1013,1015]],"rs":"202d–203a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":248,"ref":"VI.8","t":"In all souls there are two loves, but in ours five","x":"These twin Venuses and twin loves are present not only in the World Soul but also in the souls of the spheres, of the stars, of the daemons and of men. And since all souls are referred to that first soul in the fitting sequence …","tier":"speech","r":[[1013,1014]],"rs":"202d–e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":225,"ref":"VI.3","t":"On the souls of the spheres and on daemons","x":"But how daemons inhabit the middle region between heaven and earth, learn from the words of Diotima in the Symposium, of Socrates in the Phaedrus and the Philebus, and of the Athenian Stranger in the Laws and the Epinomis. Plato …","tier":"passage","r":[[1014,1015]],"rs":"202e–203a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":230,"ref":"VI.4","t":"On the seven gifts which are bestowed on men by God through the intermediate spirits","x":"The gods, who come next, are said to serve the Ideas of all things, which the divine Mind contains; the daemons are said to serve the gifts of the gods. For all things pass from the highest to the lowest through intermediaries …","tier":"speech","r":[[1014,1015]],"rs":"202e–203a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":242,"ref":"VI.7","t":"On the birth of Love","x":"But now let us return to Diotima. When she had placed Love among the daemons, for the reasons we have set out, she showed Socrates his origin in this way. On the birthday of Venus, while the gods were feasting, Porus, the son of …","tier":"passage","r":[[1016,1017]],"rs":"203b–c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":253,"ref":"VI.9","t":"What passions lovers have on account of Love's mother","x":"We have so far shown that Love is a daemon born of abundance and poverty, and that he is divided into five kinds. Next we shall treat, from the words of Diotima, what affections arise in lovers from such a nature of Love. Because …","tier":"passage","r":[[1017,1018]],"rs":"203c–d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":329,"ref":"VII.2","t":"Socrates was the true lover, and like Cupid","x":"So much for love. Let us come to Alcibiades and Socrates. After the guests had sufficiently praised the god of lovers, it remained that the lawful worshippers of this god should be praised. Socrates, all agree, loved more …","tier":"passage","r":[[1017,1020],[1075,1111]],"rs":"203c–204a (Diotima's portrait of Love), matched point by point with 215a–222b (Alcibiades on Socrates)"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":266,"ref":"VI.10","t":"What gifts lovers have on account of Love's father","x":"These things follow from Poverty, the mother of Love; their contraries follow from Abundance, his father. What the contraries are, anyone will recognise once the foregoing is understood. Above he was described as simple, unwary …","tier":"passage","r":[[1018,1022]],"rs":"203d–204c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":280,"ref":"VI.11","t":"What use love is, from its definition","x":"After Diotima has told what the origin of Love is and what he himself is like, she now reveals where he tends and what good he does to men. We all desire to have good things; and not only to have them, but to have them always. …","tier":"passage","r":[[1022,1041]],"rs":"204c–208b"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":285,"ref":"VI.12","t":"On the two loves, and that the soul is born endowed with truth","x":"Of the definition of love we have now said enough. Let us next set out the distinction concerning love which Plato draws from the fertility of the soul and of the body. The body of all men, he says, is pregnant and with child …","tier":"passage","r":[[1032,1034]],"rs":"206c–e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":293,"ref":"VI.14","t":"Whence comes love for males, whence love for women","x":"So the body of men is pregnant, as Plato holds, and the soul is pregnant too, and both are goaded to give birth by the spurs of love. But some, either by nature or by upbringing, are better fitted for the offspring of the soul …","tier":"passage","r":[[1044,1049]],"rs":"208e–209e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":296,"ref":"VI.15","t":"Above body is soul, above soul the angel, above the angel God","x":"But enough of the twofold abundance of the soul and of the twin loves. Next let us discuss by what steps Diotima raises Socrates from the lowest things to the highest. She leads him back from body to soul, from soul to angel …","tier":"passage","r":[[1050,1057]],"rs":"210a–211c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":303,"ref":"VI.16","t":"How God, angel, soul and body compare","x":"So we ascend from body to soul, from soul to angel, from angel to God. God is above eternity. The angel is wholly in eternity; for its operation as well as its essence remains stable. And rest is proper to eternity. The soul is …","tier":"speech","r":[[1050,1057]],"rs":"210a–211c"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":310,"ref":"VI.18","t":"How the soul is raised from the beauty of body to the beauty of God","x":"Come then, dearest guests, imagine Diotima addressing Socrates thus. No body, Socrates, is beautiful on every side. For either it is beautiful in this part and ugly in that; or beautiful today and at another time not at all; or …","tier":"passage","r":[[1050,1059]],"rs":"210a–211e"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":305,"ref":"VI.17","t":"How the beauties of God, angel, soul and body compare","x":"The same comparison holds between these four and their forms. The form of body consists in the composition of many parts, is bound to place, and slips away with time. The beauty of the soul suffers the changes of time, indeed …","tier":"passage","r":[[1054,1056]],"rs":"210e–211b"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":319,"ref":"VI.19","t":"How God is to be loved","x":"Thus Diotima to Socrates. But we, most distinguished gentlemen, shall not only love God without measure, as Diotima is imagined to command, but shall love God alone. For the mind stands to God as the gaze of the eyes to the sun. …","tier":"passage","r":[[1058,1060]],"rs":"211d–212a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":338,"ref":"VII.3","t":"On bestial love, that it is a form of insanity","x":"But someone may perhaps ask what good this Socratic love does the human race, why it should be celebrated with such praises, and what harm its contrary does. I shall tell you, beginning from somewhat further back. Our Plato in …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d; cites Phaedrus 265a"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":342,"ref":"VII.4","t":"Common love is a kind of fascination","x":"Now give the attention of your ears and your mind to what is to be said. In youth the blood is subtle, clear, warm and sweet. For as age advances, the subtler parts being dissolved, it becomes thicker; and for that reason it also …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":352,"ref":"VII.5","t":"How easily we are ensnared by love","x":"Can a thin ray, someone will say, a very light spirit, a very little blood of Phaedrus, so quickly, so violently, so ruinously taint the whole of Lysias? This will certainly not seem strange if you consider the other diseases …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":360,"ref":"VII.6","t":"On a strange effect of common love","x":"Shall I say what follows, most chaste friends, or shall I rather pass it over? I shall certainly say it, since the matter itself requires it, even though it seems unfit to be spoken. Yet who could speak of shameful things without …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":364,"ref":"VII.7","t":"Common love is a perturbation of the blood","x":"That this passion is in the blood is proved by this, that such a heat has no intervals of rest. Now a continuous fever is placed by the physicians in the blood; one that leaves six hours for rest, in phlegm; one that leaves a …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":366,"ref":"VII.8","t":"How lovers become like the beloved","x":"Let none of you wonder, then, if he hears that some lover has taken on in his own body a certain likeness or figure of his beloved. Pregnant women often think intensely about the wine that they most greedily crave. Intense …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":369,"ref":"VII.9","t":"By whom we are chiefly ensnared","x":"Someone will perhaps ask by whom chiefly lovers are snared, and in what way, and how they are set free. Women, certainly, capture men easily; more easily those women who display a certain masculine character; and males so much …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":373,"ref":"VII.10","t":"The manner of the ensnaring","x":"In what way lovers are fascinated we seem to have said sufficiently above, if only we add this: that mortals are most of all fascinated when, by very frequent gazing, directing the edge of sight to the edge of sight, they join …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":376,"ref":"VII.11","t":"The cure of love","x":"So far we have treated of how and by whom we are ensnared; it remains to teach in a few words how we are set free. Release is of two kinds: one by nature, the other by care. The natural release is that which is completed in fixed …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":381,"ref":"VII.12","t":"How harmful common love is","x":"But lest by speaking longer about insanity we become insane ourselves, let us conclude briefly thus. That anxious care by which common lovers are tormented day and night is a form of insanity. While love lasts they are afflicted …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d; cites Phaedrus 230e–234c and 237b–241d"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":383,"ref":"VII.13","t":"How useful divine love is, and on its four forms","x":"But by divine frenzy he is raised above the nature of man and passes into God. Divine frenzy is an illumination of the rational soul, through which God draws the soul, fallen from the things above to the things below, back from …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d; starts from Phaedrus 244a, 265a–b"},{"c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":397,"ref":"VII.16","t":"How useful Socratic love is","x":"You ask what good Socratic love does? First, it greatly helps Socrates himself to recover those wings by which he may fly back to his homeland. Then it greatly helps his city to live honourably and happily. It is not stones …","tier":"speech","r":[[1062,1118]],"rs":"212c–223d; closes on 222b"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":81,"ref":"","t":"Introduction to the Banquet","x":"The composition, says Mr. Sydenham, of this dialogue is of a singular cast, and different from that of any other. For the principal part of it consists of oratorical speeches, spoken at a certain banquet or entertainment, by some …","tier":"dialogue","r":[[860,1119]],"rs":"the whole dialogue"}],"rk":"symp","glosses":[{"s":890,"ri":51,"ph":"Eros is a great god","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":8,"ref":"I.2","n":"Ficino reads Phaedrus' 'great god' as praise drawn from Love's present excellence: he is great because, as the ancients say, all men and gods are subject to his rule.","q":"Great indeed is he to whose rule, as they say, all men and gods are subject."},{"s":890,"ri":51,"ph":"a wonder to men — and even to gods","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":8,"ref":"I.2","n":"Ficino explains why Love is called wonderful: each one loves the beauty he wonders at, the gods or angels wondering at divine beauty, men at the beauty of bodies.","q":"He is called wonderful, moreover, because each one loves that whose beauty he wonders at. The gods, or as our own writers say the angels, wonder at and love the divine beauty; men, the beauty of bodies."},{"s":891,"ri":52,"ph":"is revered as most ancient","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":9,"ref":"I.2","n":"Ficino takes the claim that Love is most ancient as Phaedrus' second praise, drawn from what went before Love: in the telling of his ancient origin Love's nobility shines out.","q":"Next Phaedrus praises him from what went before, when he declares him the most ancient of all the gods. Here Love's nobility shines out, as his ancient origin is told."},{"s":891,"ri":52,"ph":"Hesiod says that Chaos arose first","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":15,"ref":"I.3","n":"Ficino reads Chaos as the still formless essence of the Angelic Mind, and the birth of Love as that essence's first conversion towards God, before it is formed.","q":"Now that essence, not yet formed, we take to be Chaos. Its first conversion to God we call the birth of Love;"},{"s":892,"ri":54,"ph":"Earth and Love, arose after Chaos","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":20,"ref":"I.3","n":"Ficino finds three chaoses, in the Angelic Mind, the World Soul and the world's Matter, and says that in each Love comes after the chaos and before the formed world.","q":"There are, then, three worlds and three chaoses. In all of them Love attends on Chaos and goes before the world;"},{"s":892,"ri":55,"ph":"The first of all the gods he devised was Love","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":17,"ref":"I.3","n":"Ficino explains why Love precedes the gods: the Mind's appetite comes before its formation, and the gods and the world are born only in the Mind once formed.","q":"Who then will doubt that Love follows at once upon Chaos, and comes before the world and all the gods who are distributed among the parts of the world? For that appetite of the Mind is prior to its formation, and it is in the Mind once formed that the gods and the world are born."},{"s":892,"ri":56,"ph":"Shame in the case of disgraceful actions, and love of honour","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":22,"ref":"I.4","n":"Ficino sums up all Love's benefits as shunning the base and pursuing the honourable, and says love supplies shame and the desire to excel faster than laws and arts.","q":"For shame deters us from what is base, and the desire to excel spurs us to what is honourable; and nothing supplies people with these two more easily or more quickly than love."},{"s":892,"ri":56,"ph":"he is especially ashamed before his lovers","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":27,"ref":"I.4","n":"Ficino explains the point by mutual watching: lovers, each always witnessed by the other, abstain from base acts and undertake splendid ones so as to be thought worthy of love.","q":"Inasmuch as each is watched by the other, and so is never without a witness, they abstain from base things; inasmuch as they strive to please one another, they always undertake splendid things with burning zeal, so as not to be despised by the beloved but to be thought worthy of love in return."},{"s":896,"ri":59,"ph":"Pelias' daughter, Alcestis","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":28,"ref":"I.4","n":"Ficino declines to allegorise Alcestis and Orpheus, holding that these stories show love's force and dominion more powerfully when told as real events than when read as allegory.","q":"I do not intend at present to search out the allegory of Alcestis or of Orpheus; for these stories express the force and dominion of love more powerfully if they are told as things that happened than if they are thought to be said by way of allegory."},{"s":903,"ri":64,"ph":"One, the daughter of Uranus","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":66,"ref":"II.7","n":"Ficino explains Uranus, Heaven, as the highest God: as the heavenly body set on high rules and contains all bodies, so the highest God rises above all spirits.","q":"The Platonists call the highest God Heaven for this reason: just as this heaven, a body set on high, rules and contains all bodies, so that highest God rises above all spirits."},{"s":903,"ri":64,"ph":"She has no mother, and we give her the name 'Heavenly'","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":67,"ref":"II.7","n":"Ficino reads the motherless Heavenly Venus as the intelligence in the Angelic Mind: she lacks a mother because mother signifies matter, and that Mind has no share in bodily matter.","q":"The first Venus, which is in Mind, is said to be born of Heaven without a mother, because among the natural philosophers mother is matter. But that Mind is a stranger to any partnership with bodily matter."},{"s":903,"ri":64,"ph":"born of Zeus and Dione, and we call her 'Common'","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":67,"ref":"II.7","n":"Ficino reads the Common Venus as the World Soul's power of generating: her father Jupiter is the Soul's power that moves the heavens, and her mother marks her commerce with matter.","q":"Of Jupiter, that is, of that power of the Soul itself which moves the heavenly things. For it was that which created this power, which generates these lower things. They assign her a mother as well for this reason, that, being poured into the matter of the world, she is thought to have commerce with matter."},{"s":903,"ri":64,"ph":"since there are two Aphrodites, there must be two Loves","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":68,"ref":"II.7","n":"Ficino gives each Venus a love like herself: the heavenly love is seized by desire to understand God's beauty, the common love by desire to procreate that beauty in bodies.","q":"Each has as her companion a love like herself. For the former is seized by an inborn love to understand the beauty of God. The latter likewise is carried by her own love to procreate that same beauty in bodies."},{"s":905,"ri":65,"ph":"Not all Love is noble and worthy of paeans of praise","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":69,"ref":"II.7","n":"Ficino places both loves in the human soul, as the desire to contemplate beauty and the desire to generate it, and holds that each is honourable because each follows the divine image.","q":"In both, therefore, there is love. There it is the desire of contemplating beauty; here, of generating it. Each love is honourable and to be approved. For each follows the divine image."},{"s":906,"ri":66,"ph":"they love the bodies of those whom they love, rather than their souls","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":70,"ref":"II.7","n":"Ficino says Pausanias condemns not love but its abuse: neglecting contemplation for generation, excess with women, acts against nature with males, or preferring bodily form to beauty of soul.","q":"If anyone, too eager for generation, abandons contemplation, or pursues generation beyond measure with women, or against the order of nature with males, or prefers the form of the body to the beauty of the soul, he certainly abuses the dignity of love."},{"s":923,"ri":72,"ph":"the one able to contribute to intelligence and general excellence","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":83,"ref":"II.9","n":"Ficino describes the exchange as beauty for beauty: the elder enjoys the youth's bodily beauty with his eyes, while the youth attains the elder's beauty of soul with his mind.","q":"Lastly, between lovers beauty is exchanged for beauty. The man enjoys with his eyes the beauty of the younger beloved. The younger attains with his mind the beauty of the man."},{"s":927,"ri":74,"ph":"Pausanias paused","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":85,"ref":"","n":"Sydenham, quoted by Taylor, sees in this jingle Plato mimicking an Isocratean ornament, evidence that Pausanias' speech deliberately imitates the style of Isocrates, set against the Lysianic manner of Phaedrus.","q":"One of these ornaments, improperly used, Plato ridicules in the way of mimicry, as soon as the speech of Pausanias is ended: which alone seems a sufficient evidence that Plato in framing that speech purposely imitated the style of Isocrates."},{"s":929,"ri":79,"ph":"in a sense, in all of the things that are","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":86,"ref":"III.1","n":"Ficino unfolds the claim that Love is in all things through three relations: causes love their works, works seek their causes, and equals are drawn together by mutual charity.","q":"Now causes love their works as parts and images of themselves. Works in turn seek their causes as their own preservers. And those that are placed in the same order are moved towards one another by mutual charity, as like members of one and the same body."},{"s":930,"ri":80,"ph":"the Love present in a healthy body is one thing","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":97,"ref":"III.3","n":"Ficino finds Pausanias' heavenly and common loves in the body: a temperate condition loves moderate things and should be indulged; an intemperate one loves contraries and must never be obeyed.","q":"For a temperate condition of the body has a moderate love, and a love for moderate and fitting things. An intemperate condition contains the contrary love, and a love for contrary things. The former is to be indulged; the latter is never to be obeyed."},{"s":933,"ri":80,"ph":"turn the most hostile elements that are in the body into friends","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":97,"ref":"III.3","n":"Ficino takes medicine to consider how the four humours of the body become and remain friends, and which foods, drinks and practices nature loves and requires.","q":"For what else does medicine consider than how the four humours of the body may become and remain friends with one another, and which foods and drinks and which other practices of living or of healing nature loves and requires?"},{"s":935,"ri":81,"ph":"the harmony arose from the previously divergent high and low notes","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":98,"ref":"III.3","n":"Ficino explains harmony as love in music: high and low notes, different by nature, are made friendly to one another by fixed intervals and measures, and sweetness of harmony results.","q":"They make high and low notes, which are different by nature, more friendly to one another by fixed intervals and measures. From this the composition and sweetness of harmony is born."},{"s":938,"ri":82,"ph":"the Love belonging to the Heavenly Muse","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":99,"ref":"III.3","n":"Ficino assigns grave, steady melody to Urania and soft, wanton melody to Polymnia; the love of the first is to be obeyed, the appetite for the second resisted.","q":"In the Symposium he set the Muse Urania over the first and Polymnia over the second. Some love the first kind, others the second. The love of the former is to be obeyed, and the sounds they seek are to be given them; the appetite of the latter is to be resisted."},{"s":941,"ri":83,"ph":"associated with the motions of the stars and seasons of the year","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":100,"ref":"III.3","n":"Ficino finds both loves in the stars and elements that astronomy studies: moderate love when their powers sound together temperately, immoderate when one loves itself too much and abandons the others.","q":"For there is a moderate love in them when they sound together most temperately with their mutual powers. And there is an immoderate love when some one of them loves itself too much and in a way abandons the others."},{"s":942,"ri":83,"ph":"this skill is an artificer of friendship between gods and us humans","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":101,"ref":"III.3","n":"Ficino takes the art of prophets and priests to teach which human services are friendly to God, how men become God's friends, and what love is owed to God, country, parents and the dead.","q":"Lastly, the skill of prophets and priests seems to lie chiefly in this: that they teach us which services of men are friendly to God, and by what means men may become friends of God; what measure of love and charity is to be shown towards God, towards our country, towards our parents and towards others, the living as well as the dead."},{"s":947,"ri":90,"ph":"among the gods he is the best friend to humanity","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":134,"ref":"IV.6","n":"Ficino explains Love's beneficence to mankind as threefold: he leads souls to the heavenly table, fits each to its own seat, and keeps them there sweetly for ever.","q":"By his beneficence he first leads souls to the heavenly table, abounding in ambrosia and nectar; then he fits each to its own seat; lastly he keeps them there sweetly for ever."},{"s":948,"ri":91,"ph":"in times past our nature was not the same as it is now","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":115,"ref":"IV.2","n":"Ficino glosses Aristophanes' story: 'men' are souls, 'once' is their creation by God, and their wholeness is their being adorned with two lights, the inborn and the infused.","q":"Men, that is, the souls of men; once, that is, when they are created by God; are whole: they are adorned with two lights, the inborn and the infused, so that by the inborn they might behold things equal and lower, by the infused things higher."},{"s":948,"ri":91,"ph":"you should learn the nature of humanity","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":122,"ref":"IV.3","n":"Ficino argues that by 'men' Aristophanes means souls, since the name man, used of us at every age, must signify what remains stable, the soul, not the ever-flowing body.","q":"From this it can be clear that, when Aristophanes spoke of men, he meant our souls, in the Platonic manner."},{"s":951,"ri":92,"ph":"they attempted to make an ascent to heaven so as to grapple with the gods","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":115,"ref":"IV.2","n":"Ficino reads the assault on the gods as the soul's pride in turning to its inborn light alone; the splitting is its loss of the infused divine light and fall into bodies.","q":"They wished to make themselves equal to God: they turned back to the one inborn light alone. At this point they were divided: they lost the infused splendour when they were converted to the inborn alone, and at once they fell into bodies."},{"s":951,"ri":92,"ph":"the male was from the sun, the female from the earth","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":116,"ref":"IV.2","n":"Ficino reads the three sexes as souls receiving God's brightness under three virtues: the male under fortitude, the female under temperance, the mixed under justice.","q":"Some received the brightness of God according to fortitude, which is masculine; others according to temperance, which is feminine; others according to justice, which is mixed."},{"s":951,"ri":92,"ph":"seeing that the moon also partakes of male and female","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":129,"ref":"IV.5","n":"Ficino explains the parentage by giving and receiving: the Sun is male, giving light to all; the Moon mixed, taking from the Sun and giving to the elements; the Earth female.","q":"we call the Sun male, since it takes light from none and bestows it on all; the Moon mixed, from its giving and receiving, since it takes from the Sun and gives to the elements; the Earth we name female, since it takes from all and gives to none."},{"s":953,"ri":93,"ph":"I shall split them in two once more","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":115,"ref":"IV.2","n":"Ficino interprets the threatened second splitting as what befalls souls that trust too much in natural wit: even the inborn natural light left to them is in a manner put out.","q":"Grown prouder, they will be divided again: that is, if they trust too much in natural wit, that inborn and natural light which remained will in a manner be put out."},{"s":955,"ri":94,"ph":"each half, longing for the half of itself","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":116,"ref":"IV.2","n":"Ficino interprets half seeking half as the soul's surviving natural light, roused in youth, striving through the pursuit of truth to recover its lost other half, the infused divine light.","q":"souls now divided and plunged in bodies, as soon as they have come to the years of youth, are roused by the natural and inborn light which they have kept, as by a certain half of themselves, to recover by the pursuit of truth that infused and divine light, once their other half, which they lost in falling."},{"s":965,"ri":98,"ph":"the name of the desire and pursuit of the whole is Love","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":127,"ref":"IV.5","n":"Ficino identifies this Love with the mind's appetite: searching nature by its own light, the soul is spurred to recover the divine light it neglected, and that spur is true love.","q":"Therefore the mind, by the searching of its own light, is most powerfully spurred to recover the divine light. Now this spurring and appetite is true love."},{"s":965,"ri":98,"ph":"we may be sliced in two once more","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":132,"ref":"IV.5","n":"Ficino applies the second cutting to the proud who trust their own powers in divine matters, such as Diagoras or Protagoras: they darken and extinguish even the natural light left them.","q":"Therefore the souls of those who, as proud men swollen with arrogance, trust in their own powers are cut, as Aristophanes says, a second time, since they also darken with false opinions the natural light which was left to them, and put it out with wicked ways."},{"s":967,"ri":99,"ph":"he will bring us back to our original nature, heal us and make us happy","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":136,"ref":"IV.6","n":"Ficino reads this hope as three benefits: Love restores divided souls to wholeness and heaven, sets each content in its own seat, and kindles in the soul an ever new delight.","q":"To sum up briefly, then, we shall praise three benefits of Love: that by restoring us, once divided, to wholeness, he leads us back to heaven; that he sets each in his own seat and makes all at rest in that allotment; that, driving out all weariness, by a certain ardour of his own he kindles in the soul an ever new delight, as it were, and makes it blessed with a tender and sweet enjoyment."},{"s":974,"ri":112,"ph":"is the most blessed of them all, being the most beautiful and the best","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":140,"ref":"V.1","n":"Ficino asks why Agathon proves Love blessed through beauty and goodness, and answers that blessedness is perfection on every side: goodness is inward perfection, beauty outward.","q":"Now there is one perfection that is inward, and there is also one that is outward. The inward we call goodness; the outward, beauty. Therefore what is good within and also beautiful we name most blessed, as being perfect on every side."},{"s":975,"ri":113,"ph":"He is the most beautiful because he has the following characteristics","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":184,"ref":"V.7","n":"Ficino says Agathon's five traits are preparations for beauty rather than beauty itself: youth, delicacy and suppleness signify a tempered complexion; proportion and colour signify order, measure and appearance.","q":"These are preparations for a beautiful nature rather than beauty itself. For of these five parts the first three signify a tempered complexion of the body, which is the first foundation; the remaining two indicate order, measure and appearance."},{"s":975,"ri":113,"ph":"he is the youngest of the gods","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":185,"ref":"V.7","n":"Ficino takes youth in the human figure as a sign of tempered complexion, which age favours, since with time the finer humours dissolve and the thicker remain.","q":"Why young? Because this tempering is had by the favour not only of nature but also of age. For with length of time the finer parts of the humours are dissolved and the parts that remain are much thicker;"},{"s":975,"ri":113,"ph":"He constantly keeps company with the young and he himself is young","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":186,"ref":"V.7","n":"Applied to Love's power, Ficino says he is called young because the young are mostly caught in love's net, and those so caught long for the age of youth.","q":"He is called young, because the young are for the most part caught in the net of love; and those caught in his snares long for the age of youth."},{"s":976,"ri":113,"ph":"I do not agree that Eros is more ancient than Cronus and Iapetus","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":198,"ref":"V.10","n":"Ficino reconciles Phaedrus and Agathon: the love by which God creates the heavenly minds is older than they, while the love by which they turn back to their creator is younger.","q":"That love by which the heavenly ones are created we call older than they; but that by which they are drawn to their creator, younger."},{"s":976,"ri":113,"ph":"occurred through Necessity and not through Love","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":200,"ref":"V.11","n":"Ficino places Necessity's rule in the necessary procession of effects from God and their falling short of him, since no effect receives the whole goodness of its cause.","q":"For an effect never receives the whole goodness of its cause. In this necessary procession of the effect, and also in its degeneration, the rule of Necessity consists."},{"s":976,"ri":113,"ph":"the castrations, imprisonments and the other acts of violence","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":211,"ref":"V.12","n":"Ficino allegorises these: Saturn, the Angel, castrates Heaven, the highest God, by receiving his fruitfulness diminished; Jupiter, the World Soul, binds Saturn by narrowing the power received.","q":"For in this way Saturn, that is, the Angel, seems to castrate Heaven, that is, the highest God; and Jupiter too, that is, the World Soul, seems to bind Saturn, that is, to confine within narrower limits in himself, through the defect of his own nature, the power received from the Angel."},{"s":976,"ri":113,"ph":"would not have occurred had Love been among them","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":210,"ref":"V.12","n":"Ficino explains why these deeds fall under Necessity rather than Love: they happen not by the will of giver or receiver but because an effect cannot equal its cause.","q":"And this is said to happen under the reign of Necessity, because it comes about necessarily, not by the will of the giver nor of the receiver, but by that necessity by which an effect cannot be made equal to its cause."},{"s":977,"ri":114,"ph":"as well as being young he is delicate","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":184,"ref":"V.7","n":"Ficino takes Love's delicacy, in the human figure, as the smooth, firm tenderness of flesh that shows a body moderately tempered in the four humours.","q":"So an even and firm tenderness of body shows that its condition is moderate in the four humours. For this reason Agathon called him soft, delicate and tender."},{"s":978,"ri":116,"ph":"if it has a soft character he dwells there","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":186,"ref":"V.7","n":"Read as Love's power, softness for Ficino means that gentle natures are more easily taken by love, and that fierce natures, once taken, are made mild.","q":"Soft, because gentle natures are more easily taken; and those who have been taken, though fierce before, are made mild."},{"s":979,"ri":116,"ph":"he is supple in form as well","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":185,"ref":"V.7","n":"Ficino says suppleness is added so we understand Love as apt for every movement, lest his softness be taken for the languid, womanish softness of water, contrary to moderate complexion.","q":"But why nimble and supple? So that you may understand him to be apt and ready for every movement; lest perhaps you think that, when he called him soft, he meant the softness of water, womanish, languid and unfit."},{"s":979,"ri":116,"ph":"to go unnoticed as he first enters into an entire soul and then re-emerges","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":186,"ref":"V.7","n":"Read as Love's power, suppleness for Ficino means that love flows into a person secretly and flows out again in the same hidden way.","q":"Nimble and supple, because he flows in secretly and flows out in the same way."},{"s":979,"ri":116,"ph":"his measured and supple character","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":185,"ref":"V.7","n":"Ficino glosses Love's being well composed as being most honourably shaped in the order and measure of the parts, one of the preparations for beauty.","q":"After this he added \"well composed\", that is, most honourably shaped in the order and measure of the parts."},{"s":979,"ri":116,"ph":"gracelessness and Love are constantly at war with one another","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":186,"ref":"V.7","n":"Read as Love's power, proportion for Ficino means that love desires beautiful and ordered things and flees their contraries.","q":"Well-proportioned and well composed, because he desires beautiful and ordered things and flees their contraries."},{"s":980,"ri":117,"ph":"His beauty of complexion","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":185,"ref":"V.7","n":"Ficino glosses the shining complexion as a sweet appearance of colours; after these preparations Agathon is silent, leaving us to understand the coming of grace.","q":"He added also \"shining\", that is, glowing with a sweet appearance of colours. With these preparations set forth, Agathon kept silent about what remained. For it is for us to understand, after these, the coming of grace."},{"s":980,"ri":117,"ph":"this god dwells among blossoms","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":186,"ref":"V.7","n":"Read as Love's power, Ficino says he is called shining because he breathes upon a person in the flowering time of life and craves what is in flower.","q":"Shining, because he breathes upon a person's nature in the flowering and shining time of life, and craves what is in flower."},{"s":981,"ri":118,"ph":"he neither does injustice to god or man, nor suffers injustice","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":188,"ref":"V.8","n":"Ficino says Love is just because whole and true love brings an exchange of goodwill that admits no insult or injury, and this charity alone keeps mankind in peace.","q":"he is declared just, because where love is whole and true, there too is an exchange of goodwill, which admits no insult or injury."},{"s":981,"ri":118,"ph":"For if he experiences anything, he does not experience violence","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":191,"ref":"V.8","n":"Ficino takes as proof of Love's courage that all obey him and he obeys none: love arises freely in a free will, so that gifts cannot buy it nor threats compel it.","q":"For love is free, and arises of its own accord in a free will, which not even God will compel, since he decreed from the beginning that it should be free. So it comes about that Love, who brings force to bear on all, escapes the violence of all."},{"s":982,"ri":118,"ph":"Love would be pre-eminently sound-minded","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":188,"ref":"V.8","n":"Ficino says Love is temperate because he tames base cravings: seeking beauty, which consists in order and temperance, he scorns immoderate longings and shrinks from base gestures.","q":"He calls him, further, temperate, because he tames base cravings. For since Love seeks beauty, which consists in a certain order and temperance, he scorns cheap and immoderate longings, and always shrinks from base gestures;"},{"s":982,"ri":118,"ph":"not even Ares, the god of war, can stand against Love","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":189,"ref":"V.8","n":"Ficino reads this astrologically: Mars, the planet that makes men brave, is tamed by Venus, whose aspects curb his malice and check the anger he bestows.","q":"Mars surpasses the other gods, that is, the other planets, in courage, because he makes men braver. Venus tames him."},{"s":982,"ri":118,"ph":"Love possesses Ares","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":190,"ref":"V.8","n":"Ficino adds that Mars never tames Venus: Mars follows Venus, since daring is the handmaid of love; men become daring because wounded by love, not lovers because daring.","q":"Again, Mars follows Venus; Venus does not follow Mars, since daring is the handmaid of love, not love of daring."},{"s":984,"ri":119,"ph":"it is by Love's wisdom that living creatures come into being and grow","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":192,"ref":"V.8","n":"Ficino locates Love's wisdom in his being creator and preserver of all things and master of every art, as he says was argued in expounding Eryximachus.","q":"In what way Love is the creator and preserver of all things, and the master and lord of every art, I think has been discussed enough in the speech of Eryximachus. In these things the wisdom of Love is shown."},{"s":985,"ri":119,"ph":"Apollo discovered archery, medicine and prophecy","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":214,"ref":"V.13","n":"Ficino links Agathon's arts to the zodiac: the divinities presiding over the signs give the arts to the soul, Apollo through Gemini, Pallas through Aries, Vulcan through Libra.","q":"Apollo through Gemini grants prophecy, medicine and skill with the bow; Pallas through Aries, skill in weaving; Vulcan through Libra, work in bronze; and the others the remaining arts."},{"s":985,"ri":119,"ph":"under the direction of desire and Love","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":214,"ref":"V.13","n":"Ficino explains why the gods are said to give the arts through love: they lavish their gifts on men magnificently out of the kindness of their providence.","q":"And because they lavish their gifts on us magnificently out of the kindness of their providence, we say that they bestow them at the prompting of love."},{"s":985,"ri":119,"ph":"as were the Muses in music","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":215,"ref":"V.13","n":"Ficino identifies the Muses with the nine sounds of the heavens: eight tones from the eight revolving circles and a ninth harmony from them all.","q":"and that eight tones are produced by the motions of the eight circles, and from all of them together a certain ninth harmony. So we name the nine sounds of the heavens, from their musical concord, the nine Muses."},{"s":986,"ri":119,"ph":"endless calamities befell the gods, as we are told, because of the sovereignty of Necessity","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":203,"ref":"V.11","n":"Ficino settles which reign came first: absolutely, Love's rule is older, beginning in God; among created things Necessity comes first, since they proceed and degenerate before turning back by love.","q":"So, if we speak absolutely, the rule of Love is older than that of Necessity, since the power of Love begins in God, that of Necessity in created things."},{"s":986,"ri":119,"ph":"Zeus in the rulership of gods and men","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":214,"ref":"V.13","n":"Ficino says Jupiter through Leo makes a man apt for governing gods and men, which he glosses as the excellent conduct of things both divine and human.","q":"Thus Jupiter through Leo makes a man most apt for the government of gods and men, that is, for the excellent conduct of things both divine and human."},{"s":987,"ri":121,"ph":"Peace to humanity, Stillness and calm to the sea","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":195,"ref":"V.9","n":"Ficino takes Agathon's verses as the tranquillity mutual love gives not only to animals but also to the heavens and the elements, as Eryximachus showed more fully.","q":"That such tranquillity is given by mutual love not only to animals but also to the heavens and the elements, Agathon touches on here, and it was shown more fully above in the speech of Eryximachus."},{"s":988,"ri":122,"ph":"as he enchants the thought of every god and man","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":195,"ref":"V.9","n":"Ficino explains Love's soothing of the minds of gods and men with song by recalling that Love is in all things and reaches out to all things.","q":"At the close of this speech Love is said to soothe the minds of gods and men with his song. Anyone will understand this plainly who remembers that it was shown above that Love is in all things and reaches out to all things."},{"s":1008,"ri":179,"ph":"She is the one who taught me about all that belongs to love","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":219,"ref":"VI.1","n":"Ficino says Socrates claimed to have learned love from the inspired Diotima, not his human teachers, to show that only divine inspiration reveals true beauty and lawful love.","q":"He said this, in my judgement, to show that only by the inspiration of divinity can men understand what true beauty is, what lawful love is, and in what way one ought to love."},{"s":1008,"ri":179,"ph":"Love was neither beautiful nor good","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":220,"ref":"VI.1","n":"Ficino sets out the dispute: earlier speakers called Love beautiful, good, blessed and a god, Socrates and Diotima place him midway, and Ficino judges both true on different grounds.","q":"For the earlier speakers called Love beautiful, good, blessed, and a god. Socrates and Diotima deny this, and place him midway between the beautiful and the ugly, the good and the bad, the blessed and the wretched, god and man. We judge both opinions true, though each on a different ground."},{"s":1008,"ri":179,"ph":"a woman of Mantinea, Diotima","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":89,"ref":"","n":"Sydenham, via Taylor, says Socrates speaks nothing as from himself but puts his teaching into the mouth of the prophetess Diotima, to suggest it is true, divinely inspired and conveys knowledge of divine things.","q":"into whose mouth he puts what he has a mind to teach, on purpose to insinuate that his speech was indisputably true, was worthy of being thought divinely inspired, and conveyed the knowledge of divine things."},{"s":1011,"ri":187,"ph":"something between these two","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":223,"ref":"VI.2","n":"Ficino reconciles the views: love, drawn from and returning to beauty, may be called beautiful and a god; but as an affection in a kindled soul it is midway between beautiful and not beautiful.","q":"But since it is in a soul already kindled by the presence of that beautiful ray, we are compelled to call it an affection midway between the beautiful and the not beautiful."},{"s":1014,"ri":209,"ph":"A great spirit, Socrates","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":224,"ref":"VI.2","n":"Ficino says Diotima called Love a daemon because, as daemons are midway between heavenly and earthly things, Love holds the middle place between formlessness and form.","q":"It was for this reason that Diotima, to come to her now, called Love a daemon; because, just as daemons are midway between heavenly and earthly things, so Love holds the middle place between formlessness and form."},{"s":1014,"ri":209,"ph":"the entire realm of spirit lies between the divine and the mortal","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":228,"ref":"VI.3","n":"Ficino explains the intermediate daemons as rational beings dwelling in ethereal fire and air beneath the moon, immortal like the gods but passible like men.","q":"The animals that inhabit the region of ethereal fire placed beneath the moon, or of pure air, or of the misty air next to water, they call daemons. The rational animals that dwell on earth they call men. The gods, moreover, are immortal and impassible; men are passible and mortal; the daemons are indeed immortal, but passible."},{"s":1014,"ri":209,"ph":"A great spirit, Socrates","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":249,"ref":"VI.8","n":"Ficino takes the 'great' daemon as the love of the World Soul, on which all other souls' loves depend, and which everywhere rouses hearts to love.","q":"For this reason Diotima used to call these simply daemons, but that one the great daemon. He, hanging over all things throughout the whole world, does not allow hearts to grow numb, but everywhere rouses them to love."},{"s":1014,"ri":209,"ph":"A great spirit","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":68,"ref":"","n":"Hermeas contrasts the Symposium, where love is the leading theme and Plato calls it a mighty daemon binding secondary to primary natures, with the Phaedrus, which treats only love's energies.","q":"Hence in The Banquet, where love is the leading object, he delivers its middle nature, and its order, calling it a mighty dæmon, as binding secondary to primary natures."},{"s":1014,"ri":211,"ph":"Many and varied are these spirits","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":229,"ref":"VI.3","n":"Ficino identifies the good daemons, our guardians and governors of the lower world, with those Dionysius calls angels, finding no difference from Plato's meaning.","q":"The good ones, our guardians, Dionysius the Areopagite is accustomed to call by their proper name angels, governors of the lower world; and this does not differ at all from Plato's mind."},{"s":1014,"ri":211,"ph":"any commands from the gods to humanity, and their favours too","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":231,"ref":"VI.4","n":"Ficino develops the daemons' conveying role: the gifts of the divine Mind pass from highest to lowest through intermediaries, reaching men through the gods and the daemons.","q":"For all things pass from the highest to the lowest through intermediaries, so that those Ideas, the conceptions of the divine Mind, bestow their gifts on men through the gods and the daemons."},{"s":1014,"ri":211,"ph":"and Love is, indeed, among them","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":233,"ref":"VI.4","n":"Ficino reconciles Agathon and Diotima: the instinct of love, descending from God, is a god; confirmed by the Venereal daemons, it is a daemon.","q":"Because it descends from God, it is to be called a god; because it is confirmed by daemons, a daemon. So Love was rightly called a god by Agathon and a daemon by Diotima."},{"s":1015,"ri":213,"ph":"When Aphrodite was born","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":243,"ref":"VI.7","n":"Ficino reads Aphrodite's birthday as the moment when the Angelic Mind and the World Soul, the two Venuses, were arising from the supreme majesty of God.","q":"\"On the birthday of Venus\": that is, when the Angelic Mind and the World Soul, which we have called Venuses for the reason we gave elsewhere, were arising from the supreme majesty of God."},{"s":1015,"ri":213,"ph":"the gods held a feast","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":243,"ref":"VI.7","n":"Ficino reads the feasting gods as Heaven, Saturn and Jupiter feeding on their own goods: the supreme God, and essence and life in the Angel and the World Soul.","q":"\"The gods were feasting\": that is, Heaven, Saturn and Jupiter were feeding on their own goods."},{"s":1015,"ri":213,"ph":"Resource, the son of Cunning","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":244,"ref":"VI.7","n":"Ficino takes Resource as plenty, a spark or ray of the supreme God, and Cunning as God himself, called Counsel because he is the truth and goodness of all things.","q":"Porus is the son of Counsel, that is, a spark of the supreme God. For God is called Counsel and the fount of counsel, because he is the truth and the goodness of all things."},{"s":1015,"ri":213,"ph":"Poverty arrived, begging","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":244,"ref":"VI.7","n":"Ficino reads Poverty as the Angel's power of understanding in its native darkness, formless and unlit until God illumines it, like the eye before the sun rises.","q":"This power is by its own nature formless and dark unless it is illumined by God, like the power of the eye before the coming of the sun. This darkness we take to be Penia, a poverty, as it were, and a lack of light."},{"s":1015,"ri":213,"ph":"Resource was drunk on nectar","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":245,"ref":"VI.7","n":"Ficino asks why Resource is brought in drunk with nectar, and answers that by reason of the divine vitality he overflows.","q":"But why do they bring in Porus drunk with nectar? Because, by reason of the divine vitality, he overflows."},{"s":1016,"ri":213,"ph":"lies down beside him, thus conceiving Love","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":244,"ref":"VI.7","n":"Ficino explains the conception: the understanding, turned back to God, receives his ray, and the ardour kindled from its earlier darkness and that spark is love, born of poverty and plenty.","q":"This burning, this ardour, arising from the earlier darkness and the spark that kindles it, is love, born of poverty and plenty."},{"s":1016,"ri":213,"ph":"the garden of Zeus","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":245,"ref":"VI.7","n":"Ficino glosses the garden of Zeus as begetting under the shade of life, the fertility of the angelic life, since the ardour to understand arises immediately after the vigour of life.","q":"\"In the garden of Jupiter\": that is, begotten under the shade of life. For immediately after the vigour of life there arises the ardour to understand."},{"s":1016,"ri":213,"ph":"Love is also a follower and attendant of Aphrodite","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":247,"ref":"VI.7","n":"Ficino gives each of the two Venuses her own attendant love: the heavenly Venus's love thinks on the divine beauty, the common Venus's love generates that same beauty in the matter of the world.","q":"Let there be, then, two Venuses in the soul, the first heavenly, the second common. Let both have a love: the heavenly, for thinking on the divine beauty; the common, for generating that same beauty in the matter of the world."},{"s":1016,"ri":213,"ph":"Begotten at her birthday festivities","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":255,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino reads Love's birth on Venus's birthday as his being begotten together with the higher spirits he calls Venuses, so that he leads human souls back to higher things.","q":"that is, begotten together with those higher spirits which we have called Venuses, he leads the souls of men back to higher things."},{"s":1016,"ri":213,"ph":"drawn to beauty because Aphrodite is beautiful","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":255,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino explains that Love kindles souls with desire for the highest divine beauty because he arose in the spirits nearest God, most illumined by God's loveliness.","q":"that is, he kindles souls with the desire of the highest and divine beauty, since he himself arose in those spirits which, being nearest to God, are most greatly illumined by the loveliness of God, and raise us to the same rays."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"he is hard, squalid","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":256,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino takes Love's dryness and squalor as signs of his poverty: he lacks both the moisture and the heat on which all life depends, and long love makes lovers pale and lean.","q":"Diotima, to show the poverty of Love, hinted that he lacks both, moisture as well as heat, when she called him dry, lean and squalid."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"hard, squalid","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":257,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino gives a physiological reading: the lover's spirits fly out towards the beloved and are consumed, leaving thick black blood that dries the body and makes lovers melancholic.","q":"From this the body dries up and grows squalid; from this, too, lovers become melancholic."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"squalid","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":258,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino also reads the dry and the squalid as two temperaments prone to love: the dry as the melancholic, like earth, and the squalid and pale as the choleric, like fire.","q":"So by the dry and parched are understood the melancholic, in the likeness of earth; by the squalid and pale, the choleric, in the likeness of fire."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"barefoot","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":261,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino explains 'barefoot' as the lover's heedlessness: absorbed in love, he does not walk warily in the other duties of life and runs into perils like one with unprotected feet.","q":"So in his goings he runs into frequent perils, just like those who do not protect their feet with leather. And so they are hurt by frequent thorns and stones."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"always sleeping on the ground","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":261,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino glosses Plato's Greek word, rendered 'lowly', as flying along the ground through the lowest places: lovers misusing love live without sense and waste great goods on trifling cares.","q":"Plato's Greek word means flying on the ground and through the lowest places. For he saw that lovers, through the misuse of love, often live without sense, and that great goods perish through trifling cares."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"homeless","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":262,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino reads 'without a home' as a threefold exile: thought leaves the soul, the soul leaves its body, and the spirit is breathed out, each abandoning its natural home for the beloved.","q":"So thought goes out of its own house, the soul goes out, and the spirit goes out too."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"without covers","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":263,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino reads 'without covering' two ways: the lover has nowhere to rest, and he goes naked because love cannot be hidden, betrayed by gaze, speech, blushing, sighs and other outward signs.","q":"Who, again, would deny that Love wanders without covering and naked? For who could hide love, which a fierce, bull-like, fixed gaze betrays; which broken speech declares; which blushing and pallor of the face, frequent sighs, tossing of the limbs, unending complaint, untimely praises, sudden indignation, boasting, forwardness, wantonness, empty suspicion and the most menial services make known?"},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"in doorways","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":264,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino takes the doors at which Love sleeps to be the eyes and ears, the doors of the soul, where lovers linger looking at figures and listening to voices.","q":"The doors of the soul appear to be the eyes and the ears."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"or on the streets","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":264,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino reads lying on the road as stopping at bodily beauty, which should be a road leading up to higher beauty; lovers who dwell on it never reach the road's end.","q":"For the beauty of the body ought to be a kind of road by which we begin to ascend to a higher beauty."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"lying in the open air","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":265,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino explains 'under the open sky': lovers, wholly taken up with one thing, live by chance and are exposed to every peril of fortune, as the naked are to every weather.","q":"And so, living by chance, they are exposed to all the perils of fortune, as those who live naked under the open sky are exposed to all weathers."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"possessed of his mother's nature, dwelling ever alongside lack","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":265,"ref":"VI.9","n":"Ficino says Love is always needy because his origin was need, which cannot be wholly rooted out; love burns only while something remains to be attained.","q":"Indeed, since the first origin of love was from need, and what is natural cannot be wholly rooted out, Love is always needy, always thirsty."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"he has designs upon anything beautiful and good","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":267,"ref":"VI.10","n":"Ficino sets the father's gifts against the mother's defects: the same love that makes the lover careless elsewhere makes him shrewd and cunning in winning the beloved's favour.","q":"The same love which makes the lover careless and idle in other business makes him shrewd and cunning in the affairs of love, so that in wonderful ways he hunts for the favour of the beloved, whether he entangles him with wiles or wins him with services, soothes him with eloquence or charms him with song."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"a formidable hunter, always devising some schemes","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":269,"ref":"VI.10","n":"Ficino reads the hunter as the prudent philosophical lover, who tracks through bodily figures, as through footprints or scents, to the sacred loveliness of soul and gods.","q":"These, as the most skilful and most prudent of all men, philosophise in such a way that they step most providently through the figures of bodies, as through certain footprints or scents; from these they shrewdly track down the sacred loveliness of the soul and of the gods; and so, hunting prudently, they happily attain what they desire."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"because of his father","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":270,"ref":"VI.10","n":"Ficino identifies the father, Abundance, with the ray of beauty, which bends back to its source and draws the lover with it, step by step, from body up to God.","q":"for the ray of beauty, which is both Abundance and the father of Love, has this power, that it is bent back to the place from which it came, and draws the lover with it."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"he is hard, squalid","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":331,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino says Plato painted Love from the person of Socrates: lean, dry and squalid because melancholic by nature, thin from fasting and shabby from neglect.","q":"You will see him lean, dry and squalid: a man, that is, melancholic by nature, as is reported, and shaggy; thin from fasting; shabby from neglect."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"barefoot","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":331,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino matches Love's bare feet with Socrates' habit of walking without shoes, which Phaedrus attests in Plato.","q":"Walking without shoes: in Plato, Phaedrus shows that Socrates was always accustomed to walk so."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"homeless","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":332,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino matches Love's homelessness with Socrates calling himself a citizen of the world, and with his lack of hearth, soft bed, delicate fare or fine furniture.","q":"Without a home: asked of what country he was, Socrates said, \"Of the world.\" For a man's country is where the good is."},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"in doorways or on the streets","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":332,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino reads sleeping at doors and in the road, applied to Socrates, as his open heart, his delight in sight and hearing, and his untroubled readiness to lie down anywhere.","q":"Sleeping at doors, in the road, under the open sky: in our Socrates these signify an open breast and a heart lying open to all;"},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"always poor","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":333,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino matches Love's neediness with Socrates' poverty: son of a sculptor and a midwife, he earned his living carving stone and never had enough to feed his family.","q":"Always in need: who does not know that Socrates was the son of a sculptor and a midwife, that even into old age he earned his living with his own hand by carving stone,"},{"s":1017,"ri":214,"ph":"intense","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":334,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino matches Love's vehemence with Socrates' excitable nature, as Zopyrus the physiognomist judged, shown when he tossed his fingers and pulled his hair while talking.","q":"Vehement: for he was, as Zopyrus the physiognomist had rightly judged, of a most excitable nature."},{"s":1018,"ri":214,"ph":"he is neither devoid of resources nor wealthy","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":245,"ref":"VI.7","n":"Ficino explains Love's mixed state: we burn neither for what we wholly possess nor for what we wholly lack, but for a good known, judged pleasant and hoped for.","q":"Why is love partly rich and partly needy? Because we are not wont to burn either for what we possess completely or for what we wholly lack."},{"s":1018,"ri":214,"ph":"sophist","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":271,"ref":"VI.10","n":"Ficino applies the Sophist's definition: as the sophist shows the false for the true and makes men contradict themselves, so love makes lovers misjudge the beloved and contradict themselves.","q":"For lovers, blinded by the mists of love, often take false things for true, when they think their beloveds more beautiful, sharper and better than they are."},{"s":1018,"ri":214,"ph":"a clever enchanter, sorcerer","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":272,"ref":"VI.10","n":"Ficino asks why Love is a magician and answers that all magic consists in love: magic is the attraction of one thing by another through natural kinship.","q":"But why do we think Love a magician? Because the whole force of magic consists in love. The work of magic is the attraction of one thing by another from a certain kinship of nature."},{"s":1018,"ri":214,"ph":"by nature neither immortal nor mortal","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":274,"ref":"VI.10","n":"Ficino explains that Love is not mortal because two of the five loves are perpetual daemons in us, and not immortal because the three middle loves change daily.","q":"Not mortal, because two loves, those two which we call daemons, are in us perpetually. Not immortal, because the three in between them change daily, growing and diminishing."},{"s":1018,"ri":214,"ph":"neither immortal nor mortal","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":275,"ref":"VI.10","n":"Ficino gives a second reading: the inborn heat of desire is immortal because never extinguished, and mortal because it shifts from one object to another and seeks new pleasures.","q":"Immortal, because it is never put out, and changes its matter rather than vanishing. Mortal, because it does not always bend to the same thing, but, either from a change of nature or from the satiety that comes of long familiarity with the same thing, it seeks new pleasures; and when it has all but failed in one thing, it in a manner comes to life again in another."},{"s":1018,"ri":214,"ph":"the next moment he is dying; yet he comes back to life again","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":278,"ref":"VI.10","n":"Ficino reads Love's dying and reviving as the lover's alternation between hating the beloved as a thief and loving him as a mirror gleaming with heavenly brightness, so love withers and grows green again.","q":"So through this alternation it comes about that at every moment, so to speak, love withers and grows green again."},{"s":1018,"ri":214,"ph":"he is midway between wisdom and ignorance","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":279,"ref":"VI.10","n":"Ficino explains that love of wisdom neither wholly possesses nor wholly lacks wisdom: it knows its own ignorance, whereas the ignorant do not know what they lack.","q":"The love of wisdom, then, because it is partly deprived of wisdom and partly wise, is placed between wisdom and ignorance."},{"s":1018,"ri":214,"ph":"He is a life-long lover of wisdom","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":335,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino matches Love philosophising all his life with Socrates' declaration at his trial that he would rather die than cease to philosophise.","q":"Philosophising through his whole life: in that defence of his before the judges he declared to them that, if they were to acquit him of death on condition that he never again philosophise, he would rather die than cease to philosophise."},{"s":1018,"ri":214,"ph":"a clever enchanter, sorcerer, and sophist","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":336,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino applies these titles to Socrates: Alcibiades was charmed by his words more than by Marsyas and Olympus, he had a familiar daemon, and Aristophanes and his accusers called him a sophist.","q":"Enchanter, fascinator, sorcerer and sophist: for Alcibiades says that he was more charmed by the words of Socrates than by the melody of those excellent musicians Marsyas and Olympus."},{"s":1018,"ri":214,"ph":"he is midway between wisdom and ignorance","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":336,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino finds Love's middle place in Socrates, who differed from others only in knowing his own ignorance, so that he stood between wisdom and ignorance.","q":"So he stood midway between wisdom and ignorance; for though he did not know things themselves, he did know his own ignorance."},{"s":1021,"ri":217,"ph":"delicate, perfect and revered","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":279,"ref":"VI.10","n":"Ficino glosses the epithets of the supreme beauty: delicate because its sweetness draws all appetite, perfect because its rays perfect what approaches, blessed because it fills them with eternal goods.","q":"Delicate, in so far as by a certain sweetness of its own it draws the appetite of all things to itself. Perfect, in so far as it illumines with its rays the things it has drawn as they approach, and perfects them. Blessed, in so far as it fills what it has illumined with eternal goods."},{"s":1030,"ri":247,"ph":"but to have it always","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":281,"ref":"VI.11","n":"Ficino explains why we desire to have goods always: mortal goods perish, so we desire to remake them, and remaking is done by generating, whence the instinct of generation.","q":"So, in order that good things may in some way last for us always, we desire to remake those that perish. Remaking is done by generating."},{"s":1032,"ri":255,"ph":"ugliness is ill-suited to all that is divine, while beauty suits it well","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":281,"ref":"VI.11","n":"Ficino reasons that generation, a divine work making mortals like gods, is accomplished easily in the beautiful, which is akin to the divine, and shuns the ugly.","q":"Therefore generation, a divine work, is accomplished perfectly and easily in what is beautiful, and in its contrary the contrary happens."},{"s":1032,"ri":255,"ph":"all people are pregnant either in respect of the body or in respect of the soul","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":286,"ref":"VI.12","n":"Ficino takes the pregnancy of the soul to mean that it is allotted from the beginning the seeds or reasons of morals, arts and disciplines, which it brings forth when rightly tilled.","q":"So the soul was long ago allotted the reasons of morals, arts and disciplines; and from them, if it is rightly tilled, it brings forth its offspring into the light at fixed times."},{"s":1032,"ri":255,"ph":"once we attain a certain age our nature has a desire to bring forth","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":286,"ref":"VI.12","n":"Ficino illustrates bringing forth at a certain age from the body, whose inborn seeds appear at fixed turns of time as teeth, hair, beard and procreative seed.","q":"Hence, at fixed turns of time, the teeth break through, the hair comes out, the beard spreads, the seeds of procreation flow."},{"s":1034,"ri":257,"ph":"love of the begetting and the bringing forth in beauty","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":281,"ref":"VI.11","n":"Ficino defines the love of men as the desire of generation in the beautiful, for the sake of keeping life perpetual in mortal things.","q":"It is the desire of generation in the beautiful, for the sake of keeping life perpetual in mortal things."},{"s":1039,"ri":263,"ph":"forgetting is a departure of knowledge","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":282,"ref":"VI.11","n":"Ficino follows Plato in treating study and recollection as the taking up again of knowledge that is passing away, so that each piece of knowledge is preserved by renewal.","q":"For study and recollection are, as it were, the taking up again of knowledge that is passing away. Forgetting, indeed, seems to be the departure of knowledge."},{"s":1040,"ri":263,"ph":"both in body and in every other respect","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":283,"ref":"VI.11","n":"Ficino finds a love of generation in both parts of the soul: the body-ruling part breeds nourishment and offspring, the knowing part renews knowledge and begets it in writings and pupils.","q":"There is, then, in both parts of the soul, both the one that looks to knowledge and the one by which the body is ruled, an inborn love of generation for the perpetual preserving of life."},{"s":1044,"ri":266,"ph":"those who are pregnant in their souls","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":292,"ref":"VI.13","n":"Ficino locates the whole fertility of the soul in the eternal light of God shining in its inmost chambers, full of the reasons and Ideas of all things.","q":"In this, plainly, the whole fertility of the soul consists: that in its inmost chambers there shines that eternal light of God, most full of the reasons and Ideas of all things."},{"s":1044,"ri":266,"ph":"those who are pregnant in their bodies are more inclined towards women","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":294,"ref":"VI.14","n":"Ficino identifies those pregnant in body, the greater number, with followers of the common love, who seek women for the pleasure of union and bodily generation.","q":"The others do the opposite, for the sake of the pleasure of sexual union and the accomplishment of bodily generation."},{"s":1044,"ri":266,"ph":"those who are pregnant in their souls, even more than in their bodies","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":294,"ref":"VI.14","n":"Ficino says those fitter for offspring of the soul follow the heavenly love and love males nearly grown, whose sharper minds are fit for the discipline they will generate.","q":"The former, for this reason, by nature love males, and those indeed already nearly grown, rather than women or boys, because in them sharpness of mind is far more vigorous;"},{"s":1045,"ri":266,"ph":"he welcomes beautiful bodies rather than ugly ones","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":284,"ref":"VI.11","n":"Ficino explains why teachers prefer beautiful bodies: we cannot see the soul, so we conjecture from its image, the body, that a beautiful body houses a lovely soul.","q":"And so, guessing from its image, we conjecture that in a beautiful body there is a lovely soul. That is why we teach beautiful people more willingly."},{"s":1050,"ri":268,"ph":"regard the beauty in all the bodies as one and the same","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":312,"ref":"VI.18","n":"Ficino argues that the one nature of beauty shared by many bodies cannot depend on matter but on one incorporeal maker, God, working through angels and souls.","q":"Therefore the one beauty of many bodies depends on some one incorporeal maker."},{"s":1051,"ri":268,"ph":"he should become a lover of all beautiful bodies","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":313,"ref":"VI.18","n":"Ficino's Diotima tells Socrates to gather what is right in many bodies into one whole figure in his own soul, and to love that inner figure rather than any single body.","q":"So love that figure which your soul has fashioned, and the soul itself, its maker, rather than that outward one, maimed and scattered."},{"s":1051,"ri":268,"ph":"he should regard the beauty in souls as more honourable than the beauty in bodies","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":314,"ref":"VI.18","n":"Ficino says the beauty of the soul, like that of bodies, is light, namely truth, citing Plato's prayer that his soul be made beautiful as asking God for truth and wisdom.","q":"The light of the soul is truth, which alone your friend Plato seems to ask of God in his prayers."},{"s":1052,"ri":268,"ph":"the beauty present in activities and in laws","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":315,"ref":"VI.18","n":"Ficino interprets the beauty of activities and laws as the moral virtues, justice, fortitude and temperance, all sharing one truth of the purest life, to be loved first.","q":"Therefore I bid you consider first the beauty of the soul that consists in morals, so that you may understand that there is one reason of all morals, through which they are alike called honourable: namely the one truth of the purest life, which by the actions of justice, fortitude and temperance leads us to true happiness."},{"s":1052,"ri":268,"ph":"he might then see the beauty of knowledge","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":316,"ref":"VI.18","n":"Ficino places the beauty of knowledge in the intellectual virtues, wisdom, knowledge and prudence, whose single light of truth points to one higher wisdom, the beauty of the angel.","q":"And though you behold various teachings of wisdom, knowledge and prudence, yet reckon that in all of them there is a single light of truth, through which all are alike called beautiful."},{"s":1053,"ri":268,"ph":"Turning instead to the open sea of beauty","c":"Plutarch","e":"early-empire","y":100,"w":"Platonic Questions","k":"plut-moralia-4","p":1652,"ref":"Question III","n":"Plutarch cites the Symposium as Plato's teaching on love: we should turn our minds from sensible goods to things known only by the mind, enslaved to no single beauty but turned to the vast ocean of beauty.","q":"that we ought not to be enslaved by the beauty of any body, study, or learning, but laying aside such weakness, should turn to the vast ocean of beauty."},{"s":1054,"ri":269,"ph":"a beauty that is not beautiful in one respect and ugly in another","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":311,"ref":"VI.18","n":"Ficino's Diotima argues that no body is beautiful in every part, at every time or to every judge, so bodily beauty, tainted by baseness, cannot be the first and true beauty.","q":"Therefore the beauty of body, tainted by the touch of baseness, cannot be the pure, true and first beauty."},{"s":1057,"ri":270,"ph":"using these beauties as steps of ascent","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":297,"ref":"VI.15","n":"Ficino reads Diotima's steps as a fourfold ascent through the grades of nature: from body to soul, from soul to angel, from angel to God.","q":"Next let us discuss by what steps Diotima raises Socrates from the lowest things to the highest. She leads him back from body to soul, from soul to angel, from angel to God."},{"s":1059,"ri":272,"ph":"single in form","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":306,"ref":"VI.17","n":"Ficino reaches the beauty of God by subtraction: remove from bodily form matter and place, then time, then the manifold composition of forms, leaving a form altogether simple.","q":"Take away, besides, that manifold composition of forms; leave a form altogether simple: straightway you will have attained the beauty of God."},{"s":1059,"ri":272,"ph":"simple, pure and unalloyed","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":307,"ref":"VI.17","n":"Ficino likens the simple, pure beauty of God to the light remaining in the globe of the sun itself, not scattered through the air.","q":"leave a light that is one, simple and pure, like the light that stays in the very globe of the sun and is not scattered through the air: now in some measure you grasp the beauty of God."},{"s":1063,"ri":277,"ph":"extremely drunk, shouting loudly","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":90,"ref":"","n":"Sydenham, quoted by Taylor, finds Alcibiades' drunken entrance suited to his dissolute character; his speech only appears foreign to the subject and is really essential to Plato's design.","q":"He is introduced into the banquet-room, far from sober; and his behaviour and speech (for he is engaged by the company to make a speech) perfectly agree with the character of his manners."},{"s":1066,"ri":281,"ph":"you were lying here to ambush me","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":334,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino matches Love lying in wait for the beautiful and good with Alcibiades' complaint that Socrates always lay in wait for him, capturing the noble-natured for philosophy.","q":"He lies in wait for the beautiful and the good: Alcibiades says that Socrates was always lying in wait for him."},{"s":1082,"ri":304,"ph":"he is in ignorance about everything, and knows nothing","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":333,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino takes Socrates' constant profession of knowing nothing, while questioning everyone, as the poverty of his mind, matching the neediness of Love.","q":"He confessed the poverty of his own mind everywhere, questioning everyone and declaring that he himself knew nothing."},{"s":1104,"ri":319,"ph":"you yourself were more eager than the generals that I should have it rather than you","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":333,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino cites this episode as proof that Socrates was bold and fierce like Love: having won the day at Potidaea, he willingly yielded the prize to Alcibiades.","q":"Bold and fierce: how great his courage was in war Alcibiades sets out most fully in the Symposium ; and to him Socrates, though he had won the day at Potidaea, is said to have yielded the victory of his own accord."},{"s":1108,"ri":322,"ph":"he speaks of pack-asses and smiths, cobblers and tanners","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":334,"ref":"VII.2","n":"Ficino notes that although Socrates used unpolished words, as Alcibiades says, he moved his hearers more than Themistocles, Pericles and all other orators, matching Love's eloquence.","q":"Although he used unpolished words, as Alcibiades says in the Symposium , he nevertheless moved the souls of his hearers more than Themistocles and Pericles and all the other orators."},{"s":1110,"ri":323,"ph":"These, gentlemen, are my praises of Socrates","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":96,"ref":"","n":"Taylor holds Alcibiades' speech essential because it exemplifies in Socrates, an initiate in the mysteries of love, the perfection of virtue that such initiation can produce.","q":"This will be at once evident, when it is considered that the intention of Plato in it was to exemplify in the character of Socrates, as one who had been initiated in the mysteries of love, that perfection of virtue which such an initiation is capable of effecting."},{"s":1111,"ri":323,"ph":"he himself becomes more a beloved than a lover","c":"Marsilio Ficino","e":"renaissance","y":1469,"w":"On Love (De amore)","k":"ficino-de-amore","p":401,"ref":"VII.16","n":"Ficino takes this as the outcome of Socrates' useful company: having reformed Phaedo, Alcibiades, Charmides and others, he was loved by the young far more ardently than he loved.","q":"So it came about that the company of Socrates was not so much pleasant as useful, and that Socrates, as Alcibiades says, was loved by the young men much more ardently than he loved."}]});
